 journalTitle:'Notes from the darkroom.',journalLead:'Small things worth knowing before you load the film, make a print or come up to the studio.',journalCats:['DARKROOM NOTES','BEFORE YOU VISIT','FILM NOTES'],journalTitles:['Why the test strip comes first','Your first darkroom workshop','Choose the format, then slow down'],journalSummaries:['A small strip of paper can tell you more than a guessed exposure.','What to bring and what happens when the safelight comes on.','35mm, 120 or a single sheet: a different rhythm for each camera.'],readArticle:'Read the note',backJournal:'Back to journal',articles:[['A print begins with a decision. Before exposing a full sheet of paper, we test a small strip at several exposure times. Looking at the developed strip gives us a practical starting point.','One negative can produce very different prints. Exposure and contrast decisions belong to the printing process, so there is no need to get the whole sheet right on your first try.','At the workshop, you make your own test strip, compare the results and choose the exposure for your print. It is a small step that helps the rest of the process make sense.'],['Bring developed black-and-white negatives and choose a few frames you would like to see as prints. If you are unsure which frames to bring, we can look at them together.','Wear comfortable clothes and closed shoes. The studio supplies the paper, chemistry and equipment for the session. Start with the basics: the enlarger, the timer and the sequence of trays.','You do not need to arrive with darkroom experience. The point is to work through the process with your own hands and leave with something you have made.'],['A 35mm camera is easy to carry and gives you a familiar sequence of frames. A medium-format camera changes the size of the negative and often the way you look through the finder.','A large-format camera slows the process further: a tripod, a ground glass and one sheet at a time. Setting up the photograph becomes part of looking at the landscape.','There is no single right starting point. Choose a camera you want to spend time with, learn its controls and give yourself a roll — or a few sheets — to get to know it.']],
